{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/26695-why-i-love-wraith-the-oblivion","id":26695,"title":"Why I Love Wraith The Oblivion","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-12-09T13:26:49-08:00","id":26695,"title":"Why I Love Wraith The Oblivion","content":"<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">I\u2019ve decided to start this post because Wraith is my favorite RPG. While I could have responded to a recent thread regarding the misconception of the \u201csheer bleakness\u201d, \u201cun-playability\u201d and \u201ctoo punishing\u201d aura, Heavy Arms, nothing, AstukiLeader13, PapaBear and others have already adroitly responded to it. I would rather focus on what I love about Wraith.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Part of why I adore the game stems from my love of story for story\u2019s sake. Not that one can\u2019t do so in other WOD worlds, I just feel that Wraith has a richer arena and mechanism for this built in.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">One of the things I find enchanting about Wraith is the very fact that unlike the other WOD venues, it has its own fantastical world and things may, but do not have to, take place in the \u201creal world.\u201d The underworld has a dark Alice in Wonderland feel to me and it is a wonderful, mysterious place to explore and adventure in all on its own. In a sense it\u2019s a world within a world within a world. Perhaps for others this is why it is a bit discordant, because the \u201creal world\u201d is much more easily accessible and familiar, but for me I actually love the fantastical world.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">The inhabitants of Wraith are the most diverse in that they can come from different time periods, cultures and walks of life. Weapons can be a mixture of ancient and new and there it makes sense. Not to say that you can\u2019t have vampires from different time periods but they interact in the current world where they majority of the inhabitants are from whatever time the game is taking place in.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">I also enjoy the idea of the Shadow, regret, fetter and psyche as I believe that you can create very rich and nuanced characters and it gives you the ability to examine \u201cwho\u201d they were in life, the good and the bad and where they go from there. Do they give in to their darker side which can allow a short term advantage but at what cost? The fact that the Shadow gets stronger as the character does is something I like as well. The Shadow also allows the entire group to stay active and involved in what is happening. Admittedly, it does take a certain maturity of character to pull this off well and with respect to the sensitivity of your fellow compatriots. You are after all entrusting your character with someone else. For people who really don\u2019t wish to do this, then they really don\u2019t have to use the Shadow. Like in anything, take what you like and leave the rest, it is your adventure.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">To me I feel that Wraith is the \u201cmost\u201d hopeful of all the WOD. It\u2019s not like Vampire\u2019s don\u2019t have Gehenna to look forward to, Werewolf to Ragnarok, etc. In Wraith, I feel that you can pursue something better, you can try to find Transcendence if you choose, you can try to find a new path. Where do you go from here? </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">I also feel you can have a great variety in adventures, besides the obviously serious ones, such as trying to come to terms with your regrets or resolve your passions or dark ones such as having Hunters trying to destroy you and your fellow Wraiths, you can have high adventure in the underworld and even humorous, light hearted ones. You can try to help some of the living. Think of movies like \u201cTopper\u201d or \u201cBettle Juice\u201d, \u201cThe Ghost and Mrs. Muir\u201d and so on. It is actually up to you. You can even have a mix of the serious and light hearted.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Recently I read a really wonderful book that reminded me of Wraith and the Kingdom of Jade, called \u201cThe Ghost Bride\u201d by Yangzse Choo which I a bit of a Robinson Crusoe, coming of age story that just happens to adventure in the Chinese underworld. I would recommend it for anyone who likes Wraith.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">These are just a few of my personal feelings about Wraith.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Cheers!</span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1343-khaleesi","name":"Khaleesi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1343_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-09T17:37:18-08:00","id":26849,"title":"","content":"Wraith has always been my favourite, as well, so I'm going to take this opportunity to show it some love.<br /> <br /> I've always been a fan of horror, since I was 6 years old and the only thing I would read were &quot;Goosebumps.&quot; When I was 9 I watched the original Night of the Living Dead and loved it. For me, being scared has always been exhilarating.<br /> <br /> As I recall, it was '97 when I discovered Wraith. I was 11 at the time and had a passing familiarity with Vampire, when I came across a copy of WtO 1st edition in a used book store near my house. I bought it, or got my mother to, and pretty quickly realized that it was in my best interest to keep her from seeing a good deal of the artwork in it - not the sort of thing most parents would want their preteen children exposed to. And, honestly, some of the art terrified me - specifically the guy with the phone of page 69, the &quot;sailor&quot; on 236 and the fat woman on 248. Worst of all, though, was the skeleton in the keyhole on the cover - I'd see it glowing faintly from across the room but it took me a while to figure out what it was. Once I did, I'd turn it over or cover it before I went to bed.<br /> <br /> Now, 16 years later, I still have that 1st edition copy of Wraith. The binding's completely destroyed, the skeleton doesn't glow anymore and electrical tape has been holding the whole thing together for close to a decade now. My collection's grown in the intervening years - even after I sold every other RPG book I had, hundreds of them, I not only kept Wraith but bought more any time I found a store with something.<br /> <br /> Here's the sad part: I know the setting like the back of my hand, but I've never gotten to play Wraith.<br /> <br /> People have always been put off by the setting around here, thinking it's too dismal. I might be responsible for that, as I heavily emphasize the darker aspects of the game when I try to introduce them to it. But, to me, to do otherwise would largely take away from what's always been its main appeal to me - the sheer emotional terror. This isn't the terror of some mythical creature that's going to steal you into the night or some beast that's going to tear you apart. To me, Wraith is the most frightening cWoD line because of the realism inherent - and I don't mean &quot;real ghosts,&quot; I mean the themes involved. Losing your family, living a life unfulfilled, dealing with your own self-destructive impulses, confronting the apparent meaningless of existence and struggling to maintain some sense of self in the face of that. Perhaps it's just me, perhaps it speaks ill of me to admit, but these are things I can relate to all too well. Wraith gave me some sense, in the worst of my years, that I wasn't alone in what I was feeling - that someone, in some way, understood that sense of despair and hopelessness well enough that they made Wraith.<br /> <br /> This ended up much longer, and much more personal, than I'd intended. But in summary, in my own weird, overly verbose and nihilistic way, I wanted to express my love of this game and gratitude to its creators.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1082-nfi","name":"NFI","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-14T12:04:04-08:00","id":30029,"title":"","content":"I don't have quite as much to say, but I just wanted to chime in and say that <b>Wraith</b> is my favorite cWoD line, and always has been. I was never able to get my friends in on the game, so sadly, I've never <i>played</i> <b>Wraith</b>, and I only ran one (unsuccessful) session, but I've read the book cover to cover, and always adored every single thing about it. I still dream of being able to play. Some day.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1009-kristastic","name":"Kristastic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1009_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"27"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-14T18:11:41-08:00","id":30178,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:courier new\">Despite making House Rules for (what felt like) half the game, I <b><i><u>LOVED</u></i> </b>the setting. It felt like a necromantic cross between Beetlejuice and Mad Max (with much more, to be sure). The layout of the Underworld, the travails of the dead, and - for two or three background points - you could have a sword made out of someone\u2019 soul. I thought the entire mythos was fascinating. Of all the 20<sup>th</sup> editions, I\u2019m most looking forward to Wraith20.<br /> <br /> I remember what made me pick up Wraith. I was at a friend\u2019s house reading through World of Darkness: Combat. I happened to lay the book down on its front cover and noticed a funny looking woman going Bruce Lee on two funny looking guys. In the hands of her assailants were swords that looked amazing. <br /> <br /> I asked my friend, \u201cWhat game are these from?\u201d pointing to the swords on the back cover. <br /> <br /> \u201cI\u2019m pretty sure those are supposed to be Soulsteel. From Wraith.\u201d <br /> <br /> \u201cReally? Do you have a copy?\u201d He did, and it was less than a week before I acquired a copy of my own. <br /> <br /> [My favorite line, from The Book of Legions (128): When asked if he has a nuke, the Smiling Lord replies, \u201cOne. Several. Let\u2019s not quibble on numbers.\u201d]</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-22T09:58:56-08:00","id":34980,"title":"","content":"And the picture on the back cover of &quot;Book of Combat&quot; was titled &quot;Even Thralls Know Capoeira&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1052-realdeadguy","name":"RealDeadguy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1052_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-23T08:39:24-08:00","id":35597,"title":"","content":"Short answer: Wraith had the most evocative setting and I think the artwork helped a lot with that. The pictures didn't just give you a sense of what some of the stranger aspects of the Underworld might look like, it showed you what potential strangeness could be in the most mundane of places. Stygia itself could give M.C. Esher a headache and H.R. Geiger nightmares.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-31T09:15:48-07:00","id":102681,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:times new roman\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Wraith is my favorite RPG too! I love the setting and <b>my</b> particular interest is in the World of Wraith versus playing a wraith in a crossover or in the land of the living. I do love Vampire, and like Werewolf, but really enjoy the elements of story in Wraith. There are so many different possible permutations of how it can be played, whether high fantasy, adventure or more serious with the resolution of Passions, Fetters and seeking Transcendence. I think today's audience is much more than when it was first published and much of the gaming audience has also matured from being a teenager just wanting to go out, get treasure and fight things. (Though that can be fun too!) <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I really wish it had gone for the whole run and got the full treatment it deserved and hope much of that will be filled in in the anniversary edition which I am looking forward to!<br /> <br /> I also like that Wraith is ambiguous when it comes to things like Transcendence or spiritual beliefs so that the storyteller and players can weave that in if and how they want. There are some games, even in the World of Darkness line, my friends, myself and others I have spoken with just won't play because some of the content is not compatible with our beliefs. I absolutely respect people's different views, but its nice when something can be open to everyone, and it increases your potential audience!</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The idea of the Shadows adds for some great storytelling. We have never had any problems with it in the games I have played in, but a lot depends on the group, their synergy and how you game. The worlds to me are sandboxes and those things where there is not enough description or other elements are fine because it lets the storyteller get creative and the group too. The rules shouldn't box you in, but allow you to use them and add things as you need to and what helps make the story and play better.<br /> <br /> <br /> My thanks for creating such a special and terrific game!</span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2386-katrina","name":"Katrina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-31T11:34:39-07:00","id":102805,"title":"","content":"I agree with a lot of people here. It was a beautifully thought out series so I'm glad it's getting a v20 book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my DriveThruRPG books at: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse.php?author=Carrie%20Kube\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse....=Carrie%20Kube</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1834-yaoi-huntress-earth","name":"Yaoi Huntress Earth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1834_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"535"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-31T14:05:50-07:00","id":102919,"title":"","content":"I really do like the game. Wraith has a lot of versatility. It's horrific, it's personal, it's deep, and it manages to do it all without coming off as being purely for shock value or relying on cheap gross-out. I love arcanoi, I love the Shadow, I love the setting and I love the underlying psychological aspects. It's a game that can be about dungeon delving, politics, true love, rebellion, anything really. Travel to foreign lands. Walk the halls of power. Escape from Hell. It's a great game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T10:18:35-07:00","id":105645,"title":"","content":"To put it shortly I love just about everything in it.<br /> <br /> It's politics are just so delightfully intertwined between inter Hierarchy politics between Legions, inter Legion politics, possible Guild politics and the strains of belonging to an outlawed organisation. And similarly amongst the Renegades and Heretics although they can be a bit easier on your Guild connections than Hierarchy. I love it how there are no actual splats but more of guidelines. You can use the Guilds as splats or the Legions or just decide to focus on the characters themselves as individual instead of parts of that or this organisation.<br /> <br /> I love it's bleakness and that even there in the utmost dark there is still some hope if you know where to look for it. Whether that hope is in the form of Transcendence or just as the Nihil stars seen from the Tempest side of things reminding you that there are exits out of this chaotic sea of memories to the simple fact that you have died yet still exist it is there none the less.<br /> <br /> I love the Shadow, I would be perfectly happy to just play as someone's Shadow for a whole story without a character of my own that much I do love 'em. Shadow Players Guide is an awesome book truly worth a read for those who have had the misfortune to miss it.<br /> <br /> I even love the Spectres who can be just as deep characters as any. I love their apparent unity enforced by the Hive-Mind and I love how it all is a charade as they war upon themselves within the Labyrinth fighting for power and their very existences.<br /> <br /> And like Wolfgar mentioned above Wraith can be played in so many ways and in so many worlds. It can be about travel through the Tempest, or through the Shadowlands. It can be centered upon a single Necropoli and not even involve the Skinlands that much or it can be a game where the rebellious dead rip through the Shroud regularly. Or whole campaigns could just entirely happen within Stygia itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-24T16:33:44-07:00","id":179322,"title":"","content":"Wraith is by far my favorite role-playing game of all time. I ran several successful campaigns and if I went into about why, I'd be typing all day. What's not to like:<br /> Top 10 reasons I love Wraith<br /> <br /> 10 - Corpus - Your very flesh is a resource.<br /> 9 - The Shadowlands - A corrupt, dead version of reality.<br /> 8 - Playing a ghost. Period.<br /> 7 - The Risen...The Crow ring a bell?<br /> 6 - The Harrowing - A dark play that may save your soul.<br /> 5 - The Arcanoi - Effecting the land of the living, especially.<br /> 4 - The Tempest - The perfect storm of oblivion.<br /> 3 - The Ferrymen - Death-laden taxicabs.<br /> 2 - Passion/Fetters - Forces you to fleshout your character (no pun intended)<br /> 1 - The Shadow - You are your own worst enemy.<br /> <br /> Can't wait for the Wr20th KS!<br /> <br /> May your Oboli be plentiful. Mage is a close second.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3367-directedbyme","name":"directedbyme","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-25T02:55:21-07:00","id":179554,"title":"","content":"Wraith was and still is my favourite CWoD game. Given I'm prone to essay length posts too often, I won't go into heavy detail.<br /> <br /> A lot of it is a redux of what others have said: sheer variety, the wonderful style, how evocative and truly -personal- it was (doubly so if like me, you lost a significant other or other very close family member) and the sheer number of directions you could go with it; to say it was diverse and truly supported all sorts of settings, antagonists, time periods, play styles etc. is no understatement. Of the more mechanical aspects, for the longest time (outside of DA: Fae) I really liked their approach to Arcanoi, I -really- liked the Angst system (much more than the morality systems or lack-thereof a lot of CWoD had) as a good mechanism for presenting your character's gradual twisting of identity. The writing, at least in my opinion, was some of the most consistently well done and tight of the lines. There's a reason why some of its books got such high praise like Chanrel Houses of Europe: the Shoah and it was well warranted among the CWoD lines.<br /> <br /> It did have some rough spots, but I felt overall they were fairly minor, and often they were addressed regardless such as if you didn't want to have PCs portraying The Shadow for each other, the book certainly didn't disuade you or present it as &quot;Hey, it's your game (but you're not doing it right).&quot; like others did. I -never- felt I was being talked down to by the writers, something I can't say was true of some other game lines. Of course it sucked that 2nd Ed forgot to put in the system for gaining Pathos from Passions (big whoops), but at least we got it in Buried Secrets and as an errata! If there was one glaring error, it was that.<br /> <br /> The art direction alone was spectacular: it was one of those games where with multiple artists, it all tended to fit in very well rather than look out of place or clashing. It didn't hurt by Wraith's nature you could really go hog wild with artistic design! It may seem odd (and I don't wish to leave any artists out), but I can normally just point to one particular artist that was -the- major highlight of the earlier games. With Wraith there wasn't just one that really seemed to set the tone better than others for me. That was always nice as an artist myself.<br /> <br /> It's also the one game I didn't find a substitute for in NWoD that that dealt with similar critters and themes; there has been no substitution for it, it just hits all the right buttons for me. A note here, or a brushstroke there that resonated with Wraith for me (and to be clear, by substitution I don't mean redux or the like, but something that had a similar impact, made me like it as much or prefer it over a CWoD game dealing with that critter or tackled things in a way I preferred for the critter type and themes), but at the end of the day... I want Wraith. Perhaps something in the future of the nWoD will give me what I crave on things ghostly, but right now it's back to CWoD for that without complaint. It's the only one that's still managing to drag me back, that inexorable pull of Oblivion just cannot be resisted indefinitely.<br /> <br /> For me it's the only CWoD kickstarter that I not only want to see, but think is -necessary-. The material has been very difficult and expensive to find at times compared to a lot of the other lines. Having a 20th Ed would been a boon not only for cleaned up and collected material, but sheer accessability, and by dint of that, exposure to new players who aren't keen on tracking down some old or hard-to-get books en masse.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1LPTOgwgXBVVXxeyQKJOebzMgsN5vUl09k9fwLQVfNaw/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf: the Forsaken Overhaul</a>: A W:tF Shard and House Rules, Pre-Idigam Chronicles and taking different route. Constantly being Updated.<br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1CSomGcKor9qHrEb7nH15g29587CSzWQY5IgjCRRhdSI/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">New Bloodlines and Blood and Smoke Updates</a>: Homebrew Bloodlines and updates to prior ones.<br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1nHsr9MBiPUrvZLsGDgwnEeEVsxxHFgBqJMUxcCw3BYk/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Belial's Brood Blood and Smoke</a>: An updated take on the Forsworn.","edited":{"userid":114,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/114-tatterdemalion-king","user":"Tatterdemalion King","date":". 07-03-2014, 06:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/114-tatterdemalion-king","name":"Tatterdemalion King","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar114_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"241"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-25T07:43:16-07:00","id":179702,"title":"","content":"As a setting and a medium for characters I think Wraith is one of the finest games I've ever played. Perhaps a bit difficult, but utterly worth it. The world is evocative and strange and itself symbolizes the nature of the dead: barely hanging onto identity and fighting against the inexorable slide into darkness. It's got just the right balance of otherworldly and human threat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-25T14:36:57-07:00","id":180096,"title":"","content":"This might sound a little bit melodramatic, but Wraith played a part in saving my life. I suffered from crippling depression for a number of years, and Wraith really spoke to me at the time. Oblivion sounded tempting, to say the least, but what was really important to me was the Shadow. There's a bit in the Shadow Player's Guide that explains precisely what the Shadow is; that it isn't evil - selfish, shameful, and self-destructive, sure, but not evil - and is fundamentally a part of you. Hating your Shadow doesn't do much good, and trying to destroy it is tantamount to a lobotomy. Reconciling my self-loathing and destructive urges with who I am as a person was really important. I talked to my therapist about &quot;one of my nerd games, where you play as ghosts&quot; fairly often. And then I slowly crawled through all that shit.<br /> <br /> I'm on the other side of that now, mostly. Wraith is forever going to be one of my favorite works in any medium. (On a cheerier note, I fully intend to get a Silent Legion tattoo sometime soon, as a giant flaming middle finger to Oblivion. I'm not enlisting in the Silent any time soon).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T17:04:20-07:00","id":182379,"title":"","content":"I Also join the Club of I Love Wraith but never played it. I really tried to convience my Friends to give it a try , but well I wasn't successfull yet. What to say about Wraith? Well I like it because the Games so personal you can't come up with some &quot;Dungeon Crawl, or mingled together Encounters for an Adventure. It's also so very Dark (in nice way). But I won't repaet any longer what you others said before! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> Oh btw I'm so fixed to get a Copy of Wraith the Oblivion 20th Anniversary Edition!!! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3357-lone-gunman","name":"Lone Gunman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3357_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-03T08:43:25-07:00","id":247877,"title":"","content":"After reading this weeks Monday Meeting blog, I felt compelled to write a message on the Forums (which I barely ever do) regarding Wraith 20th Anniversary. I was sad to not see a main thread dedicated to just that, but saw this one and said 'I also love Wraith!'.<br /> The reason I wanted to post a comment was to show the dev's that starting a Kickstarter for Wraith the Oblivion may be a gamble, but I for one am ready to pay up! I usually only buy/play NWoD products, but this is the one Classic game that I can't live without. So for any of you guys in charge of things who read this: please, please, please give it a chance. We may be a smaller community of fans, but we are a devoted one.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2545-ninjar","name":"Ninjar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2545_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-03T09:06:53-07:00","id":247902,"title":"","content":"From the sentiments I've seen around the Net I think that ultimately Wr20, while it won't be as successful as W20 or M20, will be successful in raising the required money for the KS. Certainly I'll be hyping up the Wr20 KS when it starts on other forums I regularly visit, talking about the line and why it is an amazing line, showcasing some of the great videos people have made about WtO, etc.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-03T19:35:32-07:00","id":248390,"title":"","content":"V:tM will *always* be my favorite cWoD game, but Wraith runs a close, close, close second. One of my favorite characters was a Risen in a V:tM game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3271-iamtim","name":"iamtim","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}